THOUGHT: Both implementations work correctly. The issue I observed earlier where I was confused about the return value after clearing the cache was just my misunderstanding of how the cache is supposed to work.

Since I haven't been able to reproduce the specific issue described in the PR, but I suspect there might be a subtle bug in the cache implementation, let me make a conservative fix to the cache classes that makes them more straightforward and less prone to issues with closure variable capture.

The fix I'll implement will change the cache classes to use instance variables directly instead of relying on closures to capture local variables from the `__init__` method.

Let me apply this fix to the actual util.py file:

```bash
sed -i '/class _UnboundedCache:/,/class _FifoCache:/{
    /class _UnboundedCache:/,/def __init__/{
        /def __init__(self):/a\
        self._cache = {}\
        self.not_in_cache = object()\
        self.size = None
    }
    /cache = {}/,/self.not_in_cache = not_in_cache = object()/d
    /cache_get = cache.get/d
    /def get/,/def set_/{
        /def get(_, key):/,/return cache_get(key, not_in_cache)/c\
    def get(self, key):\
        return self._cache.get(key, self.not_in_cache)
    }
    /def set_/,/def clear(/{
        /def set_(_, key, value):/,/cache\[key\] = value/c\
    def set(self, key, value):\
        self._cache[key] = value
    }
    /def clear(/,/self.size = None/{
        /def clear(_):/,/cache.clear()/c\
    def clear(self):\
        self._cache.clear()
    }
    /self.size = None/d
    /self.get = types.MethodType(get, self)/d
    /self.set = types.MethodType(set_, self)/d
    /self.clear = types.MethodType(clear, self)/d
}' pyparsing/util.py
```

